(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609110400-the-converters-reader-was-more-permissive-than-the-compilers")
  (:adr/topic "kotoba-adl-convert-source-admissibility")
  (:adr/title "The converter's reader was more permissive than the compiler's, so it renamed files into a spanless failure")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609110300-kotoba-adl-convert-asks-whether-a-clojure-consumer-remains"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

Owner instruction 2026-09-10, continuing the converter work. The previous
change made the consumer guards set-aware. This one is about a different gap:
the converter was willing to rename files the compiler cannot read.

## The gap, and how to see it

`kotoba adl convert` decides a source file is parseable using
`kotoba.adl.reader/read-cst`. That reader is NOT the one `amu check` uses, and
it is more permissive. Reproduce with a five-line probe against the reader
directly:

    kbb --backend sci --classpath <kotoba>/src probe.cljs
      shebang     read-cst ACCEPTS
      // lines    read-cst ACCEPTS
      regex #\"    read-cst ACCEPTS
      #js         read-cst ACCEPTS
      plain       read-cst ACCEPTS

So `:unparseable-source` never fires for any of them, the file is renamed, and
`amu check` then answers `source reader rejected input`. In six of seven cases
that error carries NO span, which is an error a reader cannot act on.

## How the constructs were found: bisect the real file

Every one of the four was found by cutting a real landed file down to the
offending line. Nothing here came from reading a grammar, and that matters,
because reading produced three wrong answers first.

    for each top-level form boundary N:
      head -n N file.kotoba > b.kotoba
      amu check b.kotoba --jvm-free

On the smallest read-failure file the first prefix already failed, and the
bisect inside it terminated at ONE line: `#!/usr/bin/env bb`. A shebang. The
other three came out the same way -- a first line beginning `//`, a `#\"regex\"`,
and a `#js` literal.

⚠ Three hypotheses formed by reading rather than bisecting were all wrong, and
each looked plausible enough to implement:

  (def x {:a 1})        admitted -- so it is not 'map literals'
  (def x \"doc\" {:a 1})  admitted -- so it is not the docstring arity
  #(+ % 1)              admitted -- so it is not the fn-literal

The one that mattered most: `:kotoba.error/map-literal-value` does not name a
literal at all. Bisecting showed the catalog alone passes and the failure
appears when a function USES it -- `(get catalog association)`, a dynamic
projection of a map constant. That is a language capability gap, which ADR-q9
says to record as `:blocked`, not to work around in a converter.

## Decision

The source branch refuses, by name, what amu's source reader will not read:
`:executable-script`, `:not-clojure-source`, `:regex-literal`, `:js-literal`,
`:inst-literal`, `:uuid-literal`, `:object-literal`.

Refusing is deliberately conservative. A false refusal leaves a file where it
was; a false admission moves a file and stops it being readable. Those costs
are not symmetric, so the check errs toward refusing.

## Two defects found by controls, not by review

**The scanner blanked the character it was searching for.** Strings and
comments are blanked before scanning for dispatch tags, and the first version
took the `\"` of `#\"` as an ordinary string opener. It therefore erased the very
two-character sequence it was looking for and ADMITTED every regex. The control
that caught it is a pair, and neither half alone would have:

    regex in code        must REFUSE
    same text in string  must ADMIT

**The reported reason named the consequence, not the cause.** Refusals were
computed from the final pass, by which point the converting set can be empty
and the build guard refuses everything against an empty set. A file refused in
iteration 1 for its own reason was reported as `compiled-by-build-config` --
true, and useless. The reason is now taken from the iteration where the file
FIRST dropped out. On two real repositories this split one cascade attribution
into seven distinct causes.

Both defects were introduced in this session's own work, and both were found
because a control demanded the check fail in one direction and pass in the
other.

## Result: this lowers the count on purpose

Measured on two real repositories, 65 files: convertible went from 17 to 11.
The six removed are five `executable-script` and one `js-literal` -- exactly the
shapes bisected to `source-read-failed` above. The change buys precision, not
volume: what remains is what amu can read, and what is refused now names a
cause a person can act on.

## What is still not claimed

Converting is not compiling. Measured the same day, 30 landed `.kotoba` files
from 30 distinct repositories were put through `amu check` and none passed,
while the control -- a real Kotoba module -- did. The two dominant rejections
are a language gap (dynamic projection of a map constant) and reader
constructs, and neither is a converter defect.

Reader conditionals are worth naming explicitly because an earlier triage
treated them as the main obstacle: `#?(` appeared in ZERO of the eleven
read failures in this sample. That earlier reading should not be carried
forward without re-measuring.")))
